The root cause of the long runtime is that the planner has rewritten the query embedding the view code in an inefficient way. The suggested workaround is to use a CTE (Common Table Expression) to force the planner to materialize the selection from the views.

Case 2:
Both queries will be reduced to the same thing by the optimiser, and if that is true, I prefer the second option (`NOT IN`) as it's shorter and thus easier to parse. You should try running `EXPLAIN` on each query with real data to confirm.

Case 3:
The suggested solution is to use a single JOINed query instead of looping through multiple queries. The query can be rewritten as follows:

```sql
SELECT k.id, p.Username
FROM kills k
INNER JOIN players p ON k.killerID = p.ID
WHERE k.killText LIKE '%$gun%'
GROUP BY k.killerID, p.Username
ORDER BY tot_kills DESC;
```

Case 4:
The suggested solution is to use a single query with an INNER JOIN instead of using table variables and selecting from the functions separately. The query can be rewritten as follows:

```sql
SELECT P.ProjectName, A.Number, B.Number
FROM Project AS P
LEFT JOIN dbo.fn_ProjectNumber(@dateA) AS A ON P.ProjectID = A.ProjectID
LEFT JOIN dbo.fn_ProjectNumber(@dateB) AS B ON P.ProjectID = B.ProjectID;
```

Note that the issue was caused by referring to the wrong alias in the second LEFT JOIN.
